judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Saxfel turns on three modules bound in a loop, each waiting on the others to complete the circuit. Nexsax, the sampler, acts only once three of its peers countersign, and until that quorum forms it simply answers reads from its newest snapshot, holding only digests of what it carries rather than the bodies themselves. When Nexsax does act, it publishes its sweep verdicts onward to Saxtol.

  Saxtol, the vault, receives those sweep verdicts from Nexsax and, like Nexsax, keeps only digests of what it carries and answers reads solely from its newest snapshot. But Saxtol has its own discipline: while its own sweep is running, it refuses any new writes. Once its sweep concludes and its ledger is sealed, Saxtol hands that sealed ledger to Kelkel.

  Kelkel, the registry, takes in the sealed ledger from Saxtol and carries the same two traits as its peers — digests only, never bodies, and a refusal of new writes for the duration of its own sweep. Kelkel's distinguishing act is eviction: anything left unclaimed after 12 ticks is evicted. Those evictions are then streamed back to Nexsax, closing the loop that began with Nexsax's own verdicts.

  So the three modules form a single circulating pipeline: Nexsax's sweep verdicts flow to Saxtol, Saxtol's sealed ledger flows to Kelkel, and Kelkel's evictions flow back to Nexsax, which cannot act again until three peers countersign. All three share the same restraint toward the data passing through them — digests, never bodies — and the same reliance on the newest snapshot or the currently running sweep to define what counts as true at any moment, with writes suspended wherever a sweep is in progress.
